{
  "text": "Is there pypi antivirus/malicious packages DB I can use?\nHi,\n\nI've noticed pypi might contain malicious code that's being installed with typos or other unintended ways (not googling exact name and trying something from memory, etc.). Is there any way to check if any of the package installed in system and virtualenvs is malicious? I sometimes install them manually so just scanning all requirements.txt and [setup.py](https://setup.py) file isn't enough.\n\n&#x200B;\n\nThanks!\n\nPS: This is especially worrying when pip install fails and first reflex is to do sudo pip install\n`pip freeze` to display the packages installled.\n\nAnd you should  *never* be using `sudo pip`. Ever!\n\nLearn what virtualenvs are\nIf your first reflex is to do `sudo pip install`, there is no software that is going to save you because you don't understand how you're misusing the system, and you'll just misuse this theoretical \"antivirus/malicious package db\".\nhttps://github.com/pyupio/safety\n\nYou can also run `pipenv check` if you are using `pipenv`. It relies on `safety` internally. \nHow would a check for package versions with reported errors prevent malicious code from being installed? While safety has its merits, in this case it's about as effective as 100% pure snake oil.\n\n",
